ere's trademark  elements that are protectable and  copyrightable and there's a lot of stuff  here that I think Blue Hole is right to  be upset about but the overall genre or  idea that they're claiming to own is a  step too far in my opinion and  unfortunately for Blue Hole there was a  bit of a backlash to its initial  statement there have been plenty of  people confused and angered by Blue Hole  seemingly asserting that it owns the  Battle Royale idea that statement even  says pub G is the first standalone  Battle Royale game when titles like h1z1  king of the kill and the culling  launched in 2016  Morrison told me that Blue Hole probably  thought the internet would side with it  and quote light epic games on fire Blue  Hole has since clarified to PC gamer  that its specific problem is with the  fact that the company which developed  fortnight battle royale is also the  licensure of pub G's engine but Morrison  told me that doesn't complicate anything  legally however it is a question of  ethics Morrison says that blue hole  works directly with epic to make sure  that their game can be supported by  Unreal Engine 4 so the fact that epic  then went on to create a direct  competitor seems a little unethical but  it's also legal and Morrison says that  blue hulls public statement edged  closely to biting the hand that feeds  them of course  epic wouldn't actually stop doing  business with Blue Hole because that  would just make matters worse and in  regards the Blue Hole statement there's  one thing Morrison specifically wanted  to talk about it's the phrase  considering further action which he says  doesn't actually have to mean anything  that's something I've put in every  cease-and-desist I've ever sent out  that's what lawyers say that's what  legal teams say that's what lawyers  allow their clients to say because  there's a big difference between saying  we're going to consider further action  versus we're going to do further action  if I say answer this by Thursday or I'm  suing you I need to sue you on Thursday  if you don't answer if I say answer this  by Thursday we will consider further  legal action then I don't really have to  do anything I'm considering it I'm not  putting myself in a hole I'm allowing  the the room for more negotiation for  figuring this out for other kind of  settlements and that's what blue hole  did here however none of this  necessarily means Epic Games is in the  clear one thing the fortnight's studio  did that definitely didn't seem on the  level was mentioning pub G and marketing  materials Morrison assured me that it's  legal to compare your game to another  but blue who could use it against them  in courts  Morrison doesn't think a suit is very  likely and believes that it wouldn't be  a winning situation for blue home but  it's stuff like this that the studio  could bring up and attempt to make a  case out of it shows a judge that epic  might be trying to ride pub G's  hills and that isn't necessarily a good  start for epics defense the most  contentious part of this at least to me  is the fact that epic beat blue hole two  consoles with a free-to-play option  Morrison says that this is something  that might actually push blue hole to  file a lawsuit or try for a  court-ordered injunction  I think being first to market in this  sense it is well it's an overall concept  legally first so it's the first person  to sell a copy anywhere on anything that  is now protected on everything  everywhere but what's interesting here  is that kind of first to market race  that that fortnight seemingly is gonna  win might make blue hole actually pull  the trigger on a lawsuit or try to get  some kind of injunction again I don't  think it's going to be successful but  it's not frivolous either there is  certainly arguments to be made here  there is a game that has admitted it is  a derivative work of ours which again I  don't agree with but by their  advertising and by their similarities  there is an argument to be made there  and I think blue hole would say listen  this work is a derivative work of ours  it's infringing on our game and it needs  to be stopped before it's allowed to go  to market on these consoles and it  should be taken down off the computer  that's what an injunction is basically  these guys aren't okay with what they're  doing so they have to stop and the court  orders them to stop stop selling it stop  downloading it all of the above and  that's what blue hole would attempt to  do here despite all these ifs codes and  mites Morrison stress that this is much  more likely to be settled outside of a  courtroom blue hole and epic might not  be talking to the public about any of  this but the two companies are  definitely talking to each other in fact  we might never hear of this particular  situation again and while it's likely  we'll see more battle royale games that  are trying to ride the pub G wave it  would be surprising if Blue Hole  responded to them in the same way that  it did with fortnight and in the end I  welcome more battle royale games  competition is an incredibly important  thing it pushes people to do better  and while pub G is already an excellent  experience it could always be improved  on from the get-go for tonight's battle  royale mode looks like a simple pub G  clone but it actually does some  interesting things around building and  setting traps it definitely doesn't feel  as satisfying or rewarding when you take  down an enemy but who knows maybe  another game can come along and do  battle royale better than pub G that's  the great thing about being able to  compete over the same idea absolutely  anyone  Bitto shots  you
 

